A.6.1.1.2.2.6.1.2.2.1.2.1.2.15.1.4 reads the cap from the counterparty rather than hardcoding it — `cctp.localMinter().burnLimitsPerMessage(address(usdc))` — and A...15.1.5 loops on it:

```
while (usdcAmount > burnLimit) {
    _initiateCCTPTransfer(burnLimit, destinationDomain, mintRecipient);
    usdcAmount -= burnLimit;
}
```

A...15.1.6 then sends the remainder if non-zero. Three properties follow.

The cap is read live, so a change on Circle's side is picked up without a Grove deployment or a governance parameter update. The whole requested amount either goes or the transaction reverts, so a relayer never has to discover the cap, compute a split, and submit N transactions — which would leave partial states between them. And crucially the rate limits were already charged once, for the full `usdcAmount`, in A...15.1.2, before splitting: the accounting is denominated in economic intent while the messaging is denominated in Circle's mechanics, and the two are kept from contaminating each other.

The trade-off is gas cost that scales with the number of messages and is invisible in the function signature, plus a loop bound that depends entirely on an externally controlled value. A burn limit set pathologically low by the counterparty would turn a legitimate transfer into an out-of-gas revert — a liveness dependency, though not a safety one.
